<VI _name="lvdqmh_dlg_fill3.vi" description="Fills Delacor's running Create New DQMH Event.vi dialog and PROVES which module it will write to. Does not press OK.\0A\0ATHE MODULE RING IS SET THROUGH Value (Signaling)\2C not Ctrl Val.Set\2C and that is the whole point of this revision. The dialog rebuilds its own text when the module changes - step 6 reads 'The new event will be created in &lt;name&gt;.lvlib' - and only a Value Change event makes it do that. Ctrl Val.Set writes the number and the dialog never notices\2C so the ring would say one thing and the dialog would still be aimed somewhere else.\0A\0AAND THAT TEXT IS THE VERIFICATION. Reading the Step 6 indicator back turns an unverifiable index into a checkable fact\3A a ring index means different things depending on how the dialog was launched - from the Tools menu entry 0 is the placeholder '&lt;Select a Module&gt;'\2C from the project's right-click menu the provider pre-selects and entry 0 is a real module. Measured 2026-08-31\2C where writing index 1 on the placeholder assumption would have aimed the event at the wrong module. Step 6 names the module in words\2C so it cannot be misread.\0A\0AControls[] INDEX 0 IS THE MODULE RING and index 14 is Step 6 - measured by lvdqmh_dlg_probe.vi over all sixteen controls. Value (Signaling) is taken on {LV.Control} rather than {LV.Ring} because a reference out of Controls[] is generic and AIXML cannot express a cast to a subclass\3B the catalogue lists the property on {LV.Control} too\2C which is what makes this possible at all.">
